<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Francisco Hernández de Córdoba (Yucatán conquistador)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1517">1517</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/16th-century">16th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Francisco Hernández de Córdoba was a Spanish settler of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> who led the first European expedition to reach the Yucatán peninsula in 1517. His ships sighted the coastal town of Ecab, which the Spaniards called 'Gran <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cairo">Cairo</a>', and he was badly wounded in fighting with the Maya at Champotón. He died soon after returning to Cuba, but his reports prompted the expeditions that followed.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/francisco-hernandez-de-cordoba-yucatan-conquistador.jpg" alt="Francisco Hernández de Córdoba (Yucatán conquistador)" width="320" height="478" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Francisco_Hern%C3%A1ndez_de_C%C3%B3rdoba.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Background</h2><p>Little is known of Francisco Hernández de Córdoba before 1517. He was a native of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a> who lived in early colonial <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>, which suggests that he had taken part in the conquest of the island. He was wealthy enough to own a landed estate, including a native town, and to help finance his expedition. About 110 discontented settlers asked the governor, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diego-velazquez-de-cuellar">Diego Velázquez de Cuéllar</a>, for permission to look for new lands and resources. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bernal-diaz-del-castillo">Bernal Díaz del Castillo</a>, a member of the expedition and its fullest chronicler, says he was one of its promoters. His account also suggests that the original aim may have been to capture Indians as slaves for Cuba's farms and mines.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Course of events</h2><p>After some haggling over terms, permission was granted, and three ships under Hernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>'s command sailed on 8 February 1517. The chief pilot was Antón de Alaminos, who had sailed with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/christopher-columbus">Christopher Columbus</a>. After two days of storm and 21 days of fair weather, the crew sighted a large town near the coast, bigger than any they had seen in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a> or <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hispaniola">Hispaniola</a>. They named it the Great Cairo. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cacique">cacique</a> of the town received them peacefully, then prepared an ambush on the following day. In the Battle of Catoche the Spaniards' swords, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/crossbow">crossbows</a> and firearms put the more numerous Maya to flight. They took two prisoners, later baptised Julianillo and Melchorejo, who became the first Maya interpreters for the Spanish.</p><p>Short of water, the expedition landed at Champotón. By Bernal's account, the Maya outnumbered them and attacked at dawn, and eighty Spaniards were wounded. Hernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> was hit by twelve arrows, and two Spaniards were taken alive. Over fifty men had died, so the ships were reduced to two, one being burned at sea. The pilots chose to go by way of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/florida">Florida</a>, where a landing party found water but fought the local people. The survivors then reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/havana">Havana</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Aftermath and significance</h2><p>Hernández de <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> barely reached <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cuba">Cuba</a>. He died within days of landing, of his wounds and the thirst of the voyage, and was reportedly disappointed that Velázquez had given the next expedition to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/juan-de-grijalva">Juan de Grijalva</a>. The Spaniards had met what they regarded as an advanced civilisation, with solidly built buildings and a complex social order that they compared to the Old World's. The gold pieces the chaplain González had taken from a Maya pyramid, and Julianillo's claims that the land held gold, fed the next voyages: Grijalva's in 1518 and that of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hernan-cortes">Hernán Cortés</a> in 1519, which led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spanish-conquest-of-the-aztec-empire">Spanish conquest of the Aztec Empire</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Francisco Hernández de Córdoba (Yucatán conquistador)” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Its main town, Ecab, impressed <b>Francisco Hernández de Córdoba</b> in 1517, who called it 'Gran Cairo'.</blockquote><a href="/state/ecab">Ecab</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. Died in Sancti Spíritus, in what is now <a href="/countries/cuba">Cuba</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=1507" aria-label="Córdoba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4431 801 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Francisco Hernández de Córdoba (Yucatán conquistador)"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4881 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4881 1071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=1507">What was here in 1507? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
